hat churns out 80,000+ copies per hour. The machine and labor costs are spread across millions of units. The result: the entire production cost of a 40-page color newspaper might be only **10–20 cents**. Ink accounts for maybe 1–3 cents of that. Your home setup can’t come close because every step is hand-sized and retail-priced. ### 3. You’re not paying the real price at the newsstand Here’s the kicker: that $1.50 newspaper probably isn’t even covering its own manufacturing cost. **Advertising** pays the bills. The cover price might only cover distribution and maybe some of the paper, while display ads, classifieds, and inserts contribute the bulk of revenue. So the newspaper could sell below the raw production cost and still be profitable overall. Your home print, by contrast, has to absorb the full ink + paper cost with no ad subsidy. ### A quick reality check on your “20x” estimate If a newspaper costs $1, printing a single newspaper’s worth of pages (say 40 full-color pages) on a home inkjet could easily cost $20–$40 in ink alone. High-end inkjet cartridges yield maybe 200–300 pages at 5% coverage. At full color page coverage, usage skyrockets—some tests show a single full-page photo can consume $0.50–$1.50 of ink. Multiply by 40, and you’re in the $20–$60 range. So yes, 20x is absolutely realistic, and it’s all because you’re buying ink by the eyedropper while they buy it by the railcar. In essence, it’s the difference between buying a thimble of patented liquid from a store and swimming in a commodity ocean with advertisers footing the bill.
